@@ -1,86 +0,0 @@
-# Hookshot and LINE bridge source review
-
-## Sources inspected
-
-- Hookshot commit `57fa7f63ea904ff8e9bdebfeac2b373597bdf66a`.
-- LINE commit `9316913b1156165fa9c80f705ac8326c46fce4ef`; read its `AGENTS.md` before inspection. The accompanying LINE fork now implements the management extension described below; these findings describe the unmodified upstream revision.
-- mautrix-go commit `a6614a03769b7e0b314831af0132ab54dfbbe8f3`, the exact framework revision pinned by LINE's `go.mod`, inspected in a separate checkout.
-
-This is a source review, not proof that authenticated LINE, WhatsApp, Signal or Telegram accounts have exchanged messages. No behavior of the latter three bridges was inferred from the LINE implementation.
-
-## Hookshot foundation
-
-Keep TypeScript and Hookshot's existing Preact and `matrix-widget-api` UI. Adding another widget toolkit is unnecessary. `src/Connections/IConnection.ts` provides connection declarations, provisioning, restoration, grants, removal and update hooks. Add a messaging connection through `src/Connections/index.ts` and `type.ts`; wire configuration through `src/config/Config.ts` and sections. Extend `web/components/RoomConfigView.tsx`, `web/BridgeAPI.ts` and the existing room configuration components.
-
-`src/widgets/BridgeWidgetApi.ts` already exposes OpenID-backed widget CRUD. `src/ConnectionManager.ts:getConnectionTargets` uses an explicit switch, so target discovery needs an added case or a declaration-level discovery interface. `src/Connections/SetupConnection.ts` is the text command entry point. Both UI and commands should invoke one coordinator.
-
-Room membership, room power levels and service permissions are not account delegation. Add account-scoped manager ACL checks to listing and every mutation, including Matrix state restoration. Configure explicit Hookshot permissions: its default permits administrators across the local domain. Provisioning logs request bodies, so connection data must contain identifiers only, never bridge credentials. Connection IDs include the room ID; moving a portal requires a separate stable portal identity.
-
-## Unmodified upstream LINE capabilities
-
-| Requirement | Verified implementation | Limitation |
-| --- | --- | --- |
-| Logged-in account discovery | Framework `GET /_matrix/provision/v3/logins`, `GET /_matrix/provision/v3/whoami` | Scoped to the selected Matrix account owner |
-| Contacts and user search | Framework `/v3/contacts`, `/v3/search_users`; LINE `pkg/connector/userinfo.go:GetContactList`, `SearchUsers` | Contacts are users, not groups |
-| Existing groups | LINE `pkg/connector/sync.go:syncChatsNow` calls `GetAllChatMids(true, true)` then `GetChats` | Internal synchronization, no provisioning group-list route |
-| Group creation timestamp | `pkg/line/structs.go:Chat.CreatedTime` | Not exposed as a group discovery API; current `chatToChatInfo` does not propagate this field |
-| Existing portal lookup | Framework `Bridge.GetExistingPortalByKey`, database `Portal.GetByKey` and `GetAllWithMXID` | Internal Go APIs, not provisioning HTTP routes |
-| Bind existing group | Framework command `bridge [login ID] <chat ID>` | Command permissions, portal ownership and actual membership still apply |
-| Move existing portal | Command `bridge ... --overwrite`; internal `Portal.UpdateMatrixRoomID` | Existing command tombstones and cleans up the old Matrix room |
-| Relay configuration | Commands `set-relay` and `unset-relay`; internal `Portal.SetRelay` | No provisioning relay HTTP route |
-| Disconnect | Command `unbridge`; internal `Portal.RemoveMXID` | Existing command also calls `DeleteRoom`; unsuitable for preserving a shared room |
-
-The authoritative HTTP route registration is `bridgev2/matrix/provisioning.go:Init`. There are no general group-list, portal-list, portal-move or relay-control HTTP routes at this pinned revision. Do not implement an HTTP client against invented `/portals` or `/chats` endpoints and present it as a working mautrix integration.
-
-LINE portal keys use the raw chat MID as ID and the login ID as receiver in `syncChatsNow`. Preserve both components. Invited groups and joined groups are separate collections and must remain distinguishable. Joining an invitation is not an incidental side effect of selecting a group.
-
-## Authentication and delegation
-
-The framework provisioning middleware accepts its shared secret or configured Matrix authentication. Requests select `user_id`; explicit `login_id` selection verifies that the login belongs to that user. A manager's identity therefore cannot simply be passed as the owner of an administrator's login.
-
-The coordinator must first authenticate the manager and enforce the configured account ACL, then make a server-to-server request using the configured owner identity and stored bridge secret. Neither the owner identity nor bridge endpoint should come unchecked from browser input. Keep secrets out of room state and responses.
-
-Relay restrictions also remain relevant: `bridgev2/commands/relay.go` checks relay enablement, manager permissions, configured default relays and login ownership. In split portals, only the portal receiver can become relay. An adapter must preserve these rules rather than bypassing them through direct database writes.
-
-## Required bridge extension
-
-Add a small authenticated management extension inside the bridge process, or upstream equivalent bridgev2 support. A separate process writing the bridge database would bypass caches and locking and is not an acceptable implementation.
-
-The following is a proposed internal adapter contract, not an existing mautrix HTTP API:
-
-| Operation | Required semantics |
-| --- | --- |
-| `capabilities(accountId)` | Report independently supported discovery, connect, safe move, safe disconnect and relay capabilities |
-| `listChats(accountId, cursor)` | Return remote ID, membership, name, optional creation timestamp and its provenance, receiver ID, existing Matrix room |
-| `getPortal(accountId, chatId)` | Read authoritative mapping, relay and version from the bridge process |
-| `connect(accountId, chatId, roomId, operationId)` | Refuse an existing mapping unless explicitly handled as a move; validate permissions and room compatibility |
-| `move(accountId, chatId, expectedRoomId, targetRoomId, operationId)` | Compare expected mapping under lock, preserve the source room, update caches/state and return authoritative status |
-| `setRelay(accountId, chatId, enabled, operationId)` | Validate receiver and allowed relay policy; return persisted relay state |
-| `disconnect(accountId, chatId, expectedRoomId, operationId)` | Remove this bridge mapping and relay without deleting, tombstoning or kicking members from the shared room |
-| `getOperation(operationId)` | Report durable operation outcome for retries and reconciliation |
-
-For LINE discovery, reuse its authenticated client and existing `GetAllChatMids`/`GetChats` functions, expose joined versus invited status, and return `Chat.CreatedTime` with clear units and provenance. Group name and time matching should only rank suggestions, never silently join groups.
-
-For safe move, framework `UpdateMatrixRoomIDParams` already has independently selectable `TombstoneOldRoom` and `DeleteOldRoom` flags. Both must be false for moving one bridge out of a shared room. The existing command sets both true. Add an expected-source condition checked under the portal lock; the current `FailIfMXIDSet` boolean is insufficient for concurrency-safe moves. Reject collisions with another portal in the same bridge instance. Separate bridge instances can map to the same Matrix room; multiple portals within one instance are not generally interchangeable because the framework maps a Matrix room to one portal.
-
-For safe disconnect, call appropriate internal removal logic without command-level room cleanup, and explicitly clean only this bridge's room state. LINE synchronization eagerly creates portals for existing member groups, so a disconnected group may gain a new portal later. Define whether disconnect merely removes the shared mapping or persistently suppresses recreation, and implement a persistent filter if the latter is intended.
-
-Moving currently triggers room metadata and functional-member state updates. In the pinned framework, `UpdateMatrixRoomID` writes a singleton `ServiceMembers` list containing its own bot. For multiple bridges, reconcile that state and designate ownership of room name, topic and avatar to prevent competing updates.
-
-Persist operation intent and results, serialize mutations per portal, and reconcile bridge state after uncertain network failures. Database changes and Matrix events do not form one transaction; never report success merely because a request was sent. Test failures between mapping save, relay setup and Matrix state updates.
-
-## Real-stack acceptance checks
-
-Use real Synapse and multiple distinct bridge identities/rooms to test membership, power levels, portal moves, retained source-room usability, relay permissions, retries and restart recovery. A local fixture bridge can verify orchestration and Matrix transport but cannot prove compatibility with an authenticated external messaging service. Production adapters remain unsupported until their exact APIs and semantics have been implemented and verified against their own pinned bridge sources and accounts.
-
-## Comparison with the uploaded double-bridging guide
-
-The supplied HTML, titled "How to unite Signal and WhatsApp groups using Matrix" by Nina Buchina with James Collier, describes the same useful topology: two external groups share one Matrix room, and each network has a relay account to send messages for users without accounts on that network. Relay configuration is required on both sides. Tests must therefore cover Signal-originated messages reaching WhatsApp and WhatsApp-originated messages reaching Signal, not merely independent Matrix-to-network delivery.
-
-Its recovery steps and implementation details belong to an older bridge generation: the guide references signald and says a room-rebinding command is still planned. The inspected bridgev2 revision already implements `bridge` with explicit overwrite handling. This does not establish what any installed Signal or WhatsApp version supports; inspect and pin those bridges separately.
-
-Do not execute the guide's database repair SQL. Its `WHERE mxid = mxid` predicate matches every row with a non-null `mxid`, rather than the saved target row. It also assigns a supposed room identifier to `relay_user_id`, conflating relay-account selection with portal-room mapping. Current bridgev2 maintains additional in-memory portal caches and lifecycle state. Repair must use a verified bridge operation with an exact portal key and expected current room, not an ad hoc database update.
-
-The guide's blanket instruction to disable Matrix encryption is historical advice, not verified behavior for current bridges. An unencrypted disposable integration room can establish a transport baseline, but it proves nothing about encrypted interoperability. Advertise encrypted shared-room support only after both deployed bridge versions have passed decryption, key distribution, trust-policy and cross-bridge relay tests. Do not silently alter an existing room's encryption configuration.
-
-The guide also illustrates a bot error being forwarded to the other network. Include tests for bridge-generated notices and control commands, origin/echo deduplication and relay identity attribution, so operational failures do not create message loops or flood remote groups.
