-# Local Synapse fixture stack
-
-Run with a Python environment containing `matrix-synapse==1.162.0`:
-
-```sh
-/path/to/venv/bin/python prototypes/stack/run.py
-```
-
-The runner starts Synapse at `http://127.0.0.1:18008`, creates a manager, two Matrix bot fixture accounts, and three private rooms. Both fixtures join the shared room; each also has its own room with the manager. Six checks exercise bidirectional message delivery in the shared room and rejection of cross-room reads and writes. Synapse returns either forbidden or not-found for inaccessible event reads; writes must return forbidden.
-
-These fixtures use real Matrix HTTP endpoints. They are not external messaging bridge implementations and do not prove LINE, Signal, WhatsApp, or Telegram transport interoperability. The public test report makes that scope explicit.
-
-Configuration, signing keys, database, access tokens and logs are stored in ignored `.runtime/` with restrictive permissions. Registration requires a generated shared secret. The listener binds only to loopback, serves client endpoints only, and federation is disabled. Test rate limits are deliberately permissive. Do not use this configuration in production.
-
-Cloud execution isolates process and network namespaces between tool invocations. Start services and run their checks within the same invocation. A detached server may disappear when its invocation ends. On a regular terminal, the server remains running after tests complete. To stop it:
-
-```sh
-kill "$(cat prototypes/stack/.runtime/process.pid)"
-```
-
-Run the runner again to restart and reuse accounts and rooms. `test-report.json` contains no access tokens or passwords. To reset, stop Synapse and remove `.runtime/` before rerunning.
-
-Set `BRIDGE_STACK_RUNTIME` to an absolute directory to isolate another harness's database and credentials. Different cloud execution namespaces must never operate on the same SQLite database concurrently. `--prepare-only` creates configuration without starting the server or creating accounts.
-
-## Real Hookshot smoke test
-
-After building Hookshot, run:
-
-```sh
-/path/to/venv/bin/python prototypes/stack/hookshot-smoke.py
-```
-
-This starts Synapse and the actual compiled Hookshot appservice in the same execution, invites Hookshot into a shared room, and verifies its reply to `!hookshot help`. It uses an independent `.runtime/hookshot-stack/` database, manager-only Hookshot permissions, in-memory storage/queue, and a generated RSA key. Feed support is enabled because Hookshot requires at least one integration. No feed subscriptions or external credentials are configured. Processes stop after the smoke test. Results are written to `hookshot-test-report.json`.
