-```
-
-The combined runner prepares configurations, registers both appservices in the local Synapse configuration, starts Synapse and both bridges, runs the checks, and terminates all three processes. It must run on a dedicated prototype stack without an existing live server. Running everything in one execution also works in environments where separate tool commands have isolated network and process namespaces. The runner also initializes the Synapse configuration and fixture accounts on a fresh checkout.
-
-The runner downloads pinned official binaries with bounded retries and verifies their pinned SHA-256 hashes before execution. The report records their SHA-256 hashes, versions, room IDs, request and response event IDs, and help excerpts. Replies must come from the actual registered bridge bot, occur after the sent command in the room timeline, and contain the full help command entry. Runtime configuration, appservice secrets, SQLite databases, logs and binaries remain in ignored `.runtime/`. Each combined run uses fresh databases under `.runtime/runs/<random>/` (or `.runtime/all-three/runs/<random>/` with LINE), preventing parallel executions from sharing SQLite files. Verified release binaries are cached separately. Use `--runtime-dir` only to explicitly select another isolated path. Each test creates two new rooms. Listeners bind only to loopback. Only `@manager:bridge.test` can administer the bridges. Provisioning is enabled with generated secrets and Matrix-token fallback disabled; federation, encryption and remote logins remain disabled.
-
-Individual `prepare`, `start` and `test` actions are available for manual operation in a persistent shell. Stop those manually started bridges using their stored PIDs:
-
-```sh
-kill "$(cat prototypes/transports/.runtime/whatsapp.pid)"
-kill "$(cat prototypes/transports/.runtime/signal.pid)"
-```
-
-This is an isolated prototype, not a production deployment. `prepare` preserves existing registrations and credentials on reruns. Existing appservice registration entries are preserved. The runner refuses occupied local ports and cleans up only processes it created.
-
-## Include the Beeper LINE bridge
-
-Build the locally extended `https://github.com/beeper/line` checkout with the management API changes, then pass its executable and exact source commit:
-
-```sh
-/path/to/venv/bin/python prototypes/transports/run.py run \
-  --line-binary /absolute/path/to/line/matrix-line \
-  --line-source-sha 9316913b1156165fa9c80f705ac8326c46fce4ef
-```
-
-This creates an independent `.runtime/all-three/runs/<random>/` stack with WhatsApp, Signal and LINE appservices in two shared rooms. Bot IDs and command prefixes come from each bridge's generated configuration. `line-test-report.json` records all three executable hashes and versions, the supplied LINE source commit, and command-response event IDs. The LINE source commit argument describes build provenance; the runner cannot independently prove which source produced a supplied executable.
-
-The test checks LINE bot membership and `help` replies through real Synapse appservice transactions. It does not log in to LINE or test remote LINE messages, media, stickers, relay setup, portal moves or polling.
-
-The extended checks exercise simultaneous bot commands, reject missing and invalid provisioning tokens, and require authenticated status to honestly return no logins. For LINE, management API checks reject missing/invalid credentials, return an empty account list, and deny connect/move/set-relay/disconnect operations without a login. No mutation reaches an external network. Generated LINE management credentials are passed through `HOOKSHOT_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN`; the operation journal uses the bridge database. SQLite integrity checks run after owned processes shut down and are included in the final report.
